def E(d,disp,prec,eye,place,head,dig,src,q="",conf="verified"):
    return dict(date_iso=d,date_display=disp,precision=prec,eye=eye,place=place,headline=head,digest=dig,quote=q,confidence=conf,source_note=src)

entries=[
E("1820-02-09","Wednesday 9 February 1820","day","arbuthnot","London","Wellington: the King will sack his ministers without a divorce",
 "Ten days into the new reign, Wellington told Harriet Arbuthnot that the King meant to dismiss his ministers unless they undertook to get him a divorce. The Cabinet had refused, and she noted that the Whigs would vote solidly against any divorce. The whole trial is already here in outline, months before Caroline set out for England.",
 "Journal of Mrs Arbuthnot (1950), entry 9 Feb 1820, as summarised by The Diary Junction."),
E("1820-02-14","Monday 14 February 1820","day","greville","London","The King will not let the Queen be prayed for",
 "Greville heard that the Cabinet had sat until two in the morning. The King refused, again and again, to allow the Queen's name into the revised prayers of the Church, and nothing would move him. The question of the Liturgy would become one of the Queen's grievances and one of the crowd's slogans.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), ch. I, entry 14 Feb 1820, historyhome.co.uk."),
E("1820-06-04","Sunday 4 June 1820","day","greville","Oatlands, Surrey","Brougham gone to meet her; no one knows her intent",
 "Writing from the Duke of York's house, Greville recorded that the Queen was on her way and that Brougham had gone to meet her on the coast of France. No one in the King's circle knew what she meant to do, and Greville set down the King's behaviour at Ascot as the news came in.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 4 June 1820."),
E("1820-06-06","Tuesday 6 June 1820","day","george4","Carlton House, London","The King sends his green bags to Parliament",
 "The day after Caroline landed at Dover, the King sent a message to both Houses with sealed green bags of papers on her conduct abroad. He asked the Lords to give her conduct since leaving the kingdom their immediate and serious attention. It was the formal opening of the case against her.",
 "Message from the King, Parliamentary Archives HL/PO/JO/10/8/512 no. 218 (UK Parliament Living Heritage)."),
E("1820-06-07","Wednesday 7 June 1820","day","greville","Greenwich and London","Riding out to see the Queen come in",
 "Greville rode out on the Greenwich road to watch the Queen's entry into London. The crowds were enormous and wild with enthusiasm, and the radical Alderman Matthew Wood sat beside her in the open carriage. He closed the entry with the question everyone was asking.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 7 June 1820.",q="What will be done next? How is it to end?"),
E("1820-06-10","Saturday 10 June 1820","day","cobbett","London","The Register takes up the Queen's cause",
 "Within days of her landing Cobbett's Political Register carried its first article on the affair, addressed to the reformers. He presented the Queen's wrongs as one more face of the corrupt system the radicals were fighting, and from then on the paper became one of her loudest voices.",
 "Dating from 'The Queen Caroline Affair in Radical Periodicals', Culture (De Gruyter, 2022); content summarised from that study.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-06-16","Friday 16 June 1820","day","greville","London","Guardsmen cheer the Queen",
 "Greville recorded that a battalion of the 3rd Guards had been disciplined after refusing to give up their ball cartridges, and that drunken soldiers had been heard cheering the Queen in the streets. To a courtier this was the most frightening sign of all: the army itself seemed unsure.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 16 June 1820.",q="God save Queen Caroline!"),
E("1820-06-27","Tuesday 27 June 1820","day","greville","London","Counsel at the bar; Wilberforce jeered",
 "Against expectation the Queen's counsel were heard at the bar of the Lords, Brougham powerfully and Denman with an admired opening. Outside, a crowd abused William Wilberforce, who had tried to broker a compromise, calling him after the hypocrite in a popular play.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 27 June 1820."),
E("1820-07-05","Wednesday 5 July 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","Liverpool brings in the Bill of Pains and Penalties",
 "After a secret committee of peers reported on the green bags, Lord Liverpool introduced a bill to deprive Caroline of the title and rights of Queen and to dissolve her marriage. It charged an adulterous connection with Bartolomeo Pergami, her Italian chamberlain. The bill was read a first time and the hearing of evidence fixed for August.",
 "Lords Journals and Hansard, 5 July 1820; dating confirmed by the Wikipedia article on the bill and the De Gruyter study."),
E("1820-07-06","Thursday 6 July 1820","day","greville","London","Opinion turns; the soldiers restless",
 "After the committee's report Greville found the mood had swung: most people now expected the charges to be proved and the King to get his divorce. Yet the troops in London were showing discontent, and he recorded Henry Luttrell's dry joke about where that might lead.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 6 July 1820.",q="the extinguisher is taking fire"),
E("1820-07-19","Wednesday 19 July 1820","day","fremantle","Stanhope Street, London","The City is with her",
 "Fremantle told Buckingham that London was quieter but gloomier. The Queen's support in the City was growing, the press and some of the alehouses were openly for her, and the Lords' hearing was expected to begin on 17 August.",
 "Buckingham, Memoirs of the Court of George IV (1859), vol. 1, letter of 19 July 1820.",q="The City is completely with her"),
E("1820-08-05","Saturday 5 August 1820","day","cobbett","London","A public letter to Lord Liverpool",
 "Cobbett used his Register to address the Prime Minister directly, attacking the proceeding as a political persecution dressed up as law. The open letter to a minister was his favourite weapon, and this one was timed for the eve of the hearing.",
 "Dating from 'The Queen Caroline Affair in Radical Periodicals', Culture (De Gruyter, 2022); content in outline only.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-08-07","Monday 7 August 1820","day","caroline","Brandenburgh House, Hammersmith","The Queen writes to the King",
 "In a long open letter to her husband, Caroline reviewed a quarter century of ill-treatment, from her separation to the loss of her daughter, and protested at being tried by a bill instead of in a court of law. She warned him that the proceeding threatened the stability of his own throne. The letter appeared in The Times and was reprinted across the country; Cobbett is generally thought to have drafted it.",
 "Royal Collection Trust (RA GEO/BOX/13/83 exhibition text); Yale Law Library 'Trial by Media'.",q="sent me sorrowing through the World"),
E("1820-08-07","Monday 7 August 1820","day","creevey","Knowsley, Lancashire","Nothing to choose between them",
 "Staying with Lord Derby, Creevey gave Miss Ord his view of the Queen's case on the eve of the hearing. He had no illusions about either party to the marriage and thought there was nothing to choose between King and Queen.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 7 Aug 1820.",q="not a pin to chuse between them"),
E("1820-08-11","Friday 11 August 1820","day","fremantle","Englefield Green","The King in hiding at Windsor",
 "From his house beside Windsor Great Park, Fremantle reported that the King was living in seclusion with Lady Conyngham. Everyone was waiting to see how King and ministers would answer the Queen's letter.",
 "Buckingham, Memoirs of the Court of George IV (1859), vol. 1, letter of 11 Aug 1820.",q="living in the greatest retirement"),
E("1820-08-17","Thursday 17 August 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","The hearing opens; a motion to drop it fails",
 "With the peers called over and the Queen present, the House began the second-reading hearing. The Duke of Leinster's motion to throw the bill out at once was defeated by 206 votes to 41, and Brougham and Denman opened with objections to the whole procedure.",
 "Hansard HL 17 Aug 1820; division figures also in Creevey's letter of that day."),
E("1820-08-17","Thursday 17 August 1820","day","creevey","House of Lords","The Queen's entrance, described from the gallery",
 "Creevey wrote to Miss Ord from inside the Lords as the hearing began. He described the Queen's entrance, her veil hiding most of her face, and likened her bobbing curtsies to a Dutch toy that always rights itself. He also noted the poor reception of the Duke of York's remarks.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter headed House of Lords, 17 Aug 1820."),
E("1820-08-19","Saturday 19 August 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","The Attorney General opens the Crown's case",
 "Sir Robert Gifford set out the charges: that the Princess had lived in adultery with Pergami across Italy, Sicily and the eastern Mediterranean, at Naples, on a polacca at sea and at her villa on Lake Como. He promised that servants who had seen it would prove it.",
 "Hansard HL 19 Aug 1820; Wikipedia, Pains and Penalties Bill 1820."),
E("1820-08-21","Monday 21 August 1820","day","majocchi","House of Lords","First witness; the Queen walks out",
 "Gifford finished his opening and called the first witness, the former servant Majocchi. When he appeared at the bar the Queen rose and left the chamber. He gave his evidence in chief in Italian through the Crown's interpreter, describing the sleeping arrangements on the Princess's travels.",
 "Wikipedia (Pains and Penalties Bill 1820), citing the Lords record of 21 Aug 1820.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-08-22","Tuesday 22 August 1820","day","majocchi","House of Lords","'Non mi ricordo' under cross-examination",
 "Majocchi finished his evidence in chief, telling the Solicitor General that the Princess took breakfast with Pergami. Then Brougham cross-examined him, and on point after point he answered that he did not know or did not remember. Brougham had the interpreters state exactly what 'non mi ricordo' meant so the peers would hear it as 'I do not remember'.",
 "Hansard HL 22 Aug 1820, vol. 2 cc837–69 (witness printed as 'Majoochi').",q="I do not know; I do not remember."),
E("1820-08-29","Tuesday 29 August 1820","day","creevey","House of Lords","Brougham wins freedom to cross-examine",
 "Creevey reported that Lord Harrowby's motion allowing Brougham to cross-examine without restriction had carried by 121 to 106. He read the narrow vote as a sign of the peers' unease with the Crown's case.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), entry of 29 Aug 1820."),
E("1820-09-01","Friday 1 September 1820","range","creevey","House of Lords","The lady's maid's letters undo her",
 "Louise Demont, the Queen's former maid and one of the Crown's key witnesses, was cross-examined over two days. Creevey watched the prosecution suffer as letters she had written praising her mistress were put to her and she admitted they were hers.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letters of 1–2 Sept 1820.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-09-07","Thursday 7 September 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","The Solicitor General sums up for the Crown",
 "Sir John Copley summed up the evidence for the prosecution, asking the peers to set the servants' consistent testimony against the defence's attacks on their character. The House then prepared to adjourn so the Queen's counsel could gather witnesses.",
 "Hansard HL 7 Sept 1820, 'Summing up', vol. 2 cc1345–92."),
E("1820-09-09","Saturday 9 September 1820","day","creevey","Brooks's, London","Adjourned to October; peers angry",
 "The Lords adjourned the hearing to 3 October. Creevey reported the peers' irritation at being held in town through the autumn and the talk that the divorce clause might be dropped to save the bill.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letters of 7–9 Sept 1820."),
E("1820-09-14","Thursday 14 September 1820","day","brougham","Brougham Hall, Westmorland","Punished for praying for the Queen",
 "From home in the north, Brougham sent Creevey two stories of the country's divisions: a Scottish kirk session and a Yeomanry chaplain had both been penalised for praying for the Queen. The prayers question had reached the parishes.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), Brougham to Creevey, 14 Sept 1820."),
E("1820-09-27","Wednesday 27 September 1820","day","fremantle","Brighton","It may come down to the troops",
 "Fremantle expected rioting if the bill went forward and doubted it could pass. The Duke of York, bitterly hostile to the Queen, had said the army would be relied on and troops posted at the approaches to Parliament if needed; the government seemed alarmed.",
 "Buckingham, Memoirs of the Court of George IV (1859), vol. 1, letter of 27 Sept 1820.",q="it must eventually depend upon the troops"),
E("1820-10-03","Tuesday 3 October 1820","day","brougham","House of Lords","Brougham opens the defence",
 "Brougham began by asking the peers' indulgence, then went through the Crown's case place by place to show what the Attorney General had promised and the witnesses had failed to prove. He argued the Italian witnesses had been paid and drilled at Milan, and that Majocchi remembered everything useful and nothing awkward. Lovers as reckless as the story required were, he said, simply improbable.",
 "Hansard HL 3 Oct 1820, 'Defence of Her Majesty', vol. 3 cc112–78.",q="guilt was wary, and innocence alone unwary"),
E("1820-10-04","Wednesday 4 October 1820","day","brougham","House of Lords","The peroration: save the country",
 "Resuming next morning, Brougham finished his speech with an appeal to the peers to reject the bill for the sake of the Crown, the Church and the country itself. Contemporaries ranked it among the greatest speeches of the age; the defence witnesses followed.",
 "Hansard HL 4 Oct 1820 (text not fetched); continuation confirmed by the adjournment order at the end of the 3 Oct record and by Creevey's letters of 3–4 Oct.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-10-04","Wednesday 4 October 1820","range","creevey","House of Lords","'As good as I expected'",
 "Creevey followed Brougham's speech across the two days and gave Miss Ord a measured verdict: it was as good as he had hoped, which for Creevey, who mistrusted Brougham, was high praise.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letters of 3–4 Oct 1820.",q="as good as I expected",conf="approx"),
E("1820-10-08","Sunday 8 October 1820","day","greville","London","The finest oratory in years",
 "Greville called Brougham's speech the most magnificent display of argument and oratory in many years; even hostile peers such as Lord Lonsdale were impressed. He now expected the bill to be thrown out and the ministers to fall, while London stayed in uproar.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 8 Oct 1820."),
E("1820-10-15","Sunday 15 October 1820","day","greville","House of Lords","Every day in the gallery",
 "Attending daily, Greville found that Lieutenant Hownam's evidence for the defence had convinced many that the adultery was in fact proved, though the bill's opponents disputed it. He saw ministers running the case like a military campaign, and judged their sending away of the witness Rastelli a rash blunder that had turned against them.",
 "Greville Memoirs (1874), entry 15 Oct 1820."),
E("1820-10","October 1820","month","george4","Royal Lodge, Windsor","The King rails at the defence witnesses",
 "Following the evidence from a distance, the King wrote that he had never expected to live to see so much prevarication and lying as the defence case produced. His letters of these weeks show how closely he tracked each day's testimony.",
 "Letters of King George IV, ed. A. Aspinall (1938), p. 371, as cited in Jane Robins, The Trial of Queen Caroline (2006/07); exact day not checked.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-10-24","Tuesday 24 October 1820","day","denman","House of Lords","Denman: the Queen is the victim of perjury",
 "Denman began his summing-up for the Queen by taking apart the Naples story the Attorney General had opened with. He called Louise Demont the most dangerous witness because she built falsehood on a base of truth, and told the peers that the Queen was the victim of a conspiracy.",
 "Hansard HL 24 Oct 1820, 'Summing up', vol. 3 cc1027–95.",q="the victim of perjury and conspiracy"),
E("1820-10-25","Wednesday 25 October 1820","day","denman","House of Lords","Nero, Octavia, and 'go and sin no more'",
 "Denman closed by comparing the King's treatment of his wife to Nero's of Octavia, a barb the King never forgot. He ended with Christ's words to the woman taken in adultery, which seemed to concede guilt and was mocked in verse and print for weeks after.",
 "Creevey (letters of 21–24 Oct 1820) praises the Nero and Octavia passage; the closing scripture and its mockery are reported in later accounts. Day of the close not checked against Hansard.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-10-27","Friday 27 October 1820","day","lieven","Royal Pavilion, Brighton","The dragon chandelier and the King's whims",
 "While the Queen's counsel were attacking the King's character in the Lords, Lieven wrote to Metternich about the Pavilion. She marvelled at the dragon chandelier in the Banqueting Room, which she put at £11,000, and at the jumble of styles in a building she reckoned had cost £700,000. The letter does not mention the Queen at all.",
 "Brighton & Hove Museums, 'Dorothea von Lieven' (cites letter of 27 Oct 1820; the museum notes the chandelier actually cost £5,613).",conf="approx"),
E("1820-10-30","Monday 30 October 1820","day","creevey","Brooks's, London","The brass founders march",
 "As the peers counted heads for the coming votes, Creevey described a huge procession of the brass founders carrying addresses to the Queen. Trade after trade was marching to Hammersmith that autumn.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 30 Oct 1820."),
E("1820-11-06","Monday 6 November 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","Second reading carried by 28",
 "After the peers' own debate, led for the bill by Lord Eldon and against it by Lord Grey, the second reading passed by 123 votes to 95. The majority was far below what the government needed to face the Commons.",
 "Hansard HL 6 Nov 1820; figures confirmed in Creevey's letters and the Wikipedia article."),
E("1820-11-10","Friday 10 November 1820","day","lords-record","House of Lords","Third reading by nine; Liverpool gives up",
 "The third reading was carried by only 108 to 99. Lord Liverpool then told the House that with so narrow a majority the government would not proceed, and moved that the bill be read again in six months, the parliamentary form for killing it.",
 "Hansard HL 10 Nov 1820; Plumer Ward's diary and Creevey's letter of the same day."),
E("1820-11-10","Friday 10 November 1820","day","plumer-ward","London","The government's diarist records the climb-down",
 "Ward noted the division of 108 to 99, a majority of nine, and Liverpool's withdrawal of the bill. Days earlier he had written of weakness and bad conduct on every side; now it was over without a verdict.",
 "Plumer Ward's diary for 2 and 10 Nov 1820, as printed in Buckingham, Memoirs of the Court of George IV (1859)."),
E("1820-11-10","Friday 10 November 1820","day","creevey","Brooks's, London","Eldon's lone 'Not content'",
 "Creevey described the scene when Liverpool moved to put the bill off: the Lord Chancellor Eldon's audible 'Not content', the Duke of Clarence shouting 'Content', and the confusion of a House undoing its own work.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter headed Brooks's, 10 Nov 1820."),
E("1820-11-11","Saturday 11 November 1820","day","creevey","York Street, London","The Lords have nearly ruined themselves",
 "The morning after, Creevey summed up for Miss Ord. The bill was dead, and he thought the peers' conduct through the whole affair had come close to ruining the character of their House. London was preparing illuminations.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 11 Nov 1820."),
E("1820","1820 (autumn)","year","hone","45 Ludgate Hill, London","'Non mi ricordo!' turns the witness's words on the King",
 "Hone published a squib titled with Majocchi's answer, with Cruikshank's woodcut of the King as a bloated dandy at the bar of a court. In it the King himself answers his examiner with the witness's helpless refrain. It joined Hone's 'The Queen's Matrimonial Ladder' among the best-selling prints of the year.",
 "Morgan Library, 'Non mi ricordo!' (title woodcut, printed by and for William Hone, 1820). Day of publication not established.",conf="approx"),
E("1820-11-23","Thursday 23 November 1820","day","creevey","House of Commons","Black Rod cuts Denman off",
 "When Parliament met, Denman rose in the Commons to read a message from the Queen and was interrupted by Black Rod summoning members to the Lords for the prorogation. Creevey described the uproar and the session closed without even a speech from the throne.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 23 Nov 1820; Phillimore's letter of the same day in Buckingham's Memoirs agrees."),
E("1820-11-26","Sunday 26 November 1820","day","fremantle","Englefield Green","The King talks four hours with Grenville",
 "Fremantle reported that the King had sent for Lord Grenville and talked with him from eleven until three, low before dinner and cheerful after. With the bill gone, the question at Windsor was whether the King would change his ministers.",
 "Buckingham, Memoirs of the Court of George IV (1859), vol. 1, letter of 26 Nov 1820."),
E("1821-02-11","Sunday 11 February 1821","day","creevey","Brandenburgh House, Hammersmith","Dinner with the Queen",
 "Creevey dined with Caroline at Brandenburgh House and described the house by the river, her manner at table and her guests. The Queen of the crowds was by now an uncertain host, short of money and of allies.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), entry of 11 Feb 1821."),
E("1821-07-19","Thursday 19 July 1821","day","brougham","London","Turned away at every door of the Abbey",
 "On coronation morning Brougham wrote to Creevey that the Queen had left Brandenburgh House early and been refused entry at each door of Westminster Abbey she tried. On her way back the crowd mostly cheered her, while the King's procession met hisses mixed with cheers.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), Brougham to Creevey, 19 July 1821."),
E("1821-08-08","Wednesday 8 August 1821","day","creevey","Cantley, Yorkshire","'I am going to die'",
 "Creevey passed on Brougham's account of the Queen's last illness: she had signed her will and told Brougham plainly that she was dying. Lord Hood's letter from Brandenburgh House gave the hour of her death the night before, 7 August.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letters of 8 Aug 1821 (Creevey to Miss Ord; Hood to Brougham).",q="I am going to die, Mr. Brougham"),
E("1821-08-11","Saturday 11 August 1821","day","creevey","Cantley, Yorkshire","Did Brougham serve her well?",
 "Three days after her death Creevey let his old suspicion show: he thought Brougham had harmed the Queen by his conduct before the trial. He also recorded her wish to leave jewels to her servant Victorine, which Brougham had brushed aside.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 11 Aug 1821."),
E("1821-08-14","Tuesday 14 August 1821","day","brougham","London","Fighting the government's haste over the funeral",
 "On the day the coffin left Hammersmith for Harwich and burial in Brunswick, Brougham wrote that he and Stephen Lushington had protested at the speed the government imposed on the funeral. The route, chosen to avoid the City, was blocked by crowds that day.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), Brougham to Creevey, 14 Aug 1821; route from pasttense.co.uk."),
E("1821-09-28","Friday 28 September 1821","day","creevey","Yorkshire","Two dead on the funeral route",
 "Creevey reported the clash at the funeral, when Life Guards fired on the crowd and two men were killed. He noted the inquest's verdict of wilful murder against soldiers of the Life Guards, though no one was named.",
 "Creevey Papers (1903), letter of 28 Sept 1821 enclosing Sefton's of 13 Sept."),
]

